# A symbol has one spelling, `:name`, and no order: `.width` where a
# `Symbol` is expected is an enum variant, `<` is not defined, and a
# symbol's id stays inside the compiler (no `U32(s)`, no
# `Symbol.from_int`). Its one member is `.text`, which is read-only.

sub main()
  s: Symbol = .width
  t = :a
  print(s, t < :b, t >= :a)
  print(U32(t), Symbol.from_int(0))
  t.text = "x"
  print(t.name, t == .a)

# error: 7:16: `.width` is an enum variant, and a `Symbol` is expected; a symbol is written `:width`
# error: 9:12: operator `<` is not defined for `Symbol`: a symbol has no order; compare with `==` and `!=`
# error: 9:20: operator `>=` is not defined for `Symbol`
# error: 10:13: `U32(x)` converts a number; `x` has type `Symbol`
# error: 10:24: `Symbol` has no function `from_int`; its one function is `Symbol.from_text(word)`
# error: 11:5: cannot assign to `.text`; a symbol's name is read-only
# error: 12:11: a `Symbol` has no field `name`; its one member is its name, `.text`
# error: 12:23: `.a` is an enum variant, and a `Symbol` is expected; a symbol is written `:a`
